From 9bad88626ca7028b21b9a608db9f8084000f799f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cpkt9762 <cpkt9762@163.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 16:55:48 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] feat(cloud): serve over TLS and allow dropping the dashboard

Forward-port of fork patch 0009. Upstream grew a real dashboard since
47f281c, so the server half needed restructuring rather than transplanting.

Server side:

- WithTLS routes Start through ListenAndServeTLS. A half-configured pair is
  ignored rather than fatal, so setting only the cert keeps the listener on
  plain HTTP instead of refusing to boot.
- WithoutDashboard / WithDashboardFromEnv (ENGRAM_CLOUD_DASHBOARD=0) drop the
  browser surface. Everything under /sync and /admin is behind withAuth, but
  the dashboard bootstrap and login pages answer before authentication, so on
  a published listener they are the entire pre-auth surface.

The dashboard is now ~75 lines spanning dashboard.Mount plus /dashboard/
bootstrap and eight /dashboard/admin/* routes, so it moved into its own
mountDashboard method and the guard wraps the call. Wrapping only the
HandleFunc calls -- the shape the original patch's smaller diff suggested --
would have left dashboard.Mount registering /dashboard, /dashboard/login and
the whole browser surface while the bootstrap route returned 404, which looks
correct from the outside.

TestWithoutDashboardDropsPreAuthRoutes was extended to probe the
Mount-registered paths for exactly that reason; the original version only
checked /dashboard/bootstrap and would have passed against the broken shape.
Mutation-checked: neutering the guard fails the test on /dashboard. /sync/pull
and /health are asserted to survive.

Client side: upstream added newRemoteHTTPClient with a redirect guard that
refuses to carry a bearer token to a non-HTTPS hop. Rather than replace it
with this patch's newHTTPClient, the CA handling is merged into it, so both
protections apply. The constructors now propagate its error, since a bad
ENGRAM_CLOUD_CA_FILE must fail loudly instead of silently falling back to the
system pool.

ENGRAM_CLOUD_CA_FILE trusts exactly one certificate for this client. The
usual alternative for a self-signed deployment is installing the CA as a
system trust root, which needs sudo, applies machine-wide, and turns a leaked
CA key into a man-in-the-middle licence for every HTTPS connection on the
box.

Full suite: the set of failing packages is byte-identical to the pristine
upstream/main baseline (cmd/engram, internal/server, internal/setup,
internal/version, plugin -- all pre-existing environment failures, verified
in a clean worktree). The only difference is the added cloudcrypto package,
which passes.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
